.TH "LE" "1" "Aug 2026" "le" ""

.SH NAME
le-stop - Stop a listener (by port/pid) or every listener under a directory


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBle stop [port|pid] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Stop a listener on a port (or the process with a PID), each via its
recommended strategy (TERM / brew services / docker / launchctl). The
PID's start time is re-verified immediately before any signal, so a
recycled PID is never the one that gets signalled — and when identity
can't be proven, le refuses and asks you to rescan instead of guessing.

.PP
With --dir, stop every listener whose working directory is that path or
nested under it — the terminal equivalent of the app's folder-stop, for
clearing out everything a project spun up.

.PP
Use --dry-run to see exactly what would be stopped without touching
anything — worth doing before a --dir sweep. --json emits the per-
listener outcomes (or the dry-run preview) as an array for scripts.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-d\fP, \fB--dir\fP=""
	stop every listener whose working directory is under this path

.PP
\fB-n\fP, \fB--dry-run\fP[=false]
	print what would be stopped without stopping it

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for stop

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output per-listener results as JSON


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBle(1)\fP


